    <!-- Sculpt context -->
    <!--
      The one destructive act in the product, named by what it does to the
      user's work rather than by the mechanism: it throws the current sculpt
      away and starts again from the Construction shape. The ellipsis is the
      promise that it will ask first, and it does.
    -->
    <string name="reset_sculpt_from_shape">Reset Sculpt from Shape…</string>
    <!--
      The same destructive act for an Imported Mesh (IMPORT-01B).

      "from Shape" would be Construction-only wording over a body that has no
      shape behind it. The SOURCE it rebuilds from is the imported geometry,
      which the import kept and sculpting never wrote, so the name says that
      instead. The guard, the ellipsis and the confirmation are unchanged.
    -->
    <string name="reset_sculpt_from_imported_mesh">Reset Sculpt from Imported Mesh…</string>
    <string name="reset_sculpt_confirm_message_imported">Resetting rebuilds the sculpt mesh from the imported mesh. The sculpting already made on this mesh will be discarded. This cannot be undone.</string>
    <string name="sculpt_mesh_summary">Sculpt mesh: %1$d vertices · %2$s</string>
    <string name="sculpt_gesture_rule">One finger on the model — %1$s; elsewhere it orbits.</string>
    <string name="stale_source_warning">The Construction shape changed after you started sculpting. Your sculpt is kept as it is — Reset Sculpt from Shape to start again from the new shape.</string>

    <!-- The destructive reset's confirmation. Names the consequence, and the
         button carries the verb rather than "OK". -->
    <string name="reset_sculpt_confirm_title">Replace the sculpted mesh?</string>
    <string name="reset_sculpt_confirm_message">Resetting rebuilds the sculpt mesh from the current Construction shape. The sculpting already made on this mesh will be discarded. This cannot be undone.</string>
    <string name="reset_sculpt_confirm_action">Discard and reset</string>
    <string name="cancel">Cancel</string>

    <!--
      Status messages.

      Every one of these is a TRANSIENT: it reports something that just happened
      and then gets out of the way. The one message that stands until the user
      acts on it is `stale_source_warning`, which is a state rather than a
      verdict. There is deliberately no ambient "Showing the current box in m."
      any more — a line that always says something says nothing, and it spent a
      capsule at the top of the model saying what the panel title and the unit
      chips already said.

      Nor is any of them INSTRUCTIONAL. Two were: one written on entering
      Construction and one on every Shape/Transform switch, both naming the
      workflow rather than reporting an event, and between them they put a
      sentence across the top of the viewport in every resting Construction
      screenshot the review saw. What they said is already answered by the
      surfaces the user is looking at — the held rail entry names the context,
      the precision surface titles itself "Exact Shape — Body #1", and the
      Objects capsule names the body. A verdict earns the capsule; a caption
      does not.
    -->
